WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:04.595
Picture a café with free Wi-Fi.

2
00:00:04.595 --> 00:00:10.339
You buy a train ticket on your laptop, and you see the little padlock next to the web address.

3
00:00:10.339 --> 00:00:11.317
Safe, you think.

4
00:00:11.533 --> 00:00:14.103
Then a friend asks a fair question.

5
00:00:14.103 --> 00:00:15.205
Safe from what?

6
00:00:15.205 --> 00:00:16.673
Hold on to that one.

7
00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:22.342
Between your laptop and the ticket site sit a lot of machines you don't own.

8
00:00:22.342 --> 00:00:24.954
The café's Wi-Fi, routers, your provider.

9
00:00:24.954 --> 00:00:27.184
Each one passes your message along.

10
00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:29.790
Plain web traffic is a postcard.

11
00:00:29.790 --> 00:00:33.152
It's readable text, the language called HTTP.

12
00:00:33.367 --> 00:00:42.056
So whoever runs one of those machines, or simply shares the café's open Wi-Fi, can read your password, or even change the page.

13
00:00:42.967 --> 00:00:45.005
What we want is a sealed envelope.

14
00:00:45.005 --> 00:00:51.961
Nobody on the way can read it, nobody can change it without being caught, and we know who opens it at the other end.

15
00:00:52.167 --> 00:00:55.574
To seal anything, both ends need the same secret key.

16
00:00:55.574 --> 00:00:56.731
Here's the puzzle.

17
00:00:56.731 --> 00:01:01.552
How do two machines agree on a key, when everyone in the café is listening?

18
00:01:01.767 --> 00:01:04.655
The obvious answer: just send the key across.

19
00:01:04.655 --> 00:01:06.196
Let's see how that goes.

20
00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:06.740
Well.

21
00:01:06.740 --> 00:01:11.981
Now everyone on the way has a copy of the key, and the seal is worth nothing.

22
00:01:12.700 --> 00:01:13.856
Here's the trick.

23
00:01:13.856 --> 00:01:17.730
Each side picks a random secret of its own, and keeps it.

24
00:01:17.730 --> 00:01:18.885
It never travels.

25
00:01:19.100 --> 00:01:23.563
From its secret, each side makes a public value, and sends only that.

26
00:01:23.563 --> 00:01:26.409
Capital A goes one way, capital B the other.

27
00:01:26.633 --> 00:01:28.312
The café sees A and B.

28
00:01:28.312 --> 00:01:29.761
That's all it sees.

29
00:01:29.967 --> 00:01:34.879
Now each side combines its own secret with the other side's public value.

30
00:01:34.879 --> 00:01:37.167
Your laptop mixes little a with B.

31
00:01:37.167 --> 00:01:39.253
The site mixes little b with A.

32
00:01:39.253 --> 00:01:41.407
And both arrive at the same key.

33
00:01:41.633 --> 00:01:44.885
The public values alone are not enough to work it out.

34
00:01:44.885 --> 00:01:48.559
It feels like a magic trick, and it's the kind that holds up.

35
00:01:48.767 --> 00:01:51.471
From here on, the request travels sealed.

36
00:01:51.471 --> 00:01:54.241
The café sees an envelope, not a postcard.

37
00:01:54.867 --> 00:01:55.436
But wait.

38
00:01:55.436 --> 00:01:57.082
A sealed envelope to whom?

39
00:01:57.082 --> 00:02:02.780
Someone in the café could play the same trick with you, and pretend to be the ticket site.

40
00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:06.868
So the seal is only worth something if you know who holds the other end.

41
00:02:07.100 --> 00:02:09.050
So the site sends a certificate.

42
00:02:09.050 --> 00:02:15.327
It's a signed statement that says: this public key belongs to these names, from this date to that date.

43
00:02:15.533 --> 00:02:20.157
And it sends a signature that only the matching private key can make.

44
00:02:20.157 --> 00:02:23.440
That's the same proof your SSH key gave in Day 7.

45
00:02:24.967 --> 00:02:26.991
But who signed the certificate?

46
00:02:26.991 --> 00:02:28.951
A certificate authority, a CA.

47
00:02:28.951 --> 00:02:37.637
Your browser can't ask you about every site, so it trusts a few CAs to vouch for them, the way a passport office vouches for a photo.

48
00:02:37.867 --> 00:02:44.033
Usually an intermediate CA signs the server's certificate, and a root CA signs the intermediate.

49
00:02:44.033 --> 00:02:47.245
That lets the root keys stay offline, locked away.

50
00:02:47.467 --> 00:02:53.944
Your operating system and browser ship with a trust store, a short list of root CAs they already trust.

51
00:02:53.944 --> 00:02:58.723
The client follows the chain up, link by link, until it reaches one of them.

52
00:02:58.933 --> 00:03:01.184
It matches, so the padlock appears.

53
00:03:01.184 --> 00:03:06.520
If any link is missing, expired, or made out to another name, the connection fails.

54
00:03:06.733 --> 00:03:08.880
You'll see that failure in the lab.

55
00:03:08.880 --> 00:03:15.319
A certificate you make yourself for localhost is signed by itself, so curl refuses it, and it's right to.

56
00:03:15.319 --> 00:03:17.527
It can't tell it from an attacker's.

57
00:03:17.733 --> 00:03:23.877
With --cacert localhost.crt, you tell curl to trust exactly that one certificate.

58
00:03:23.877 --> 00:03:26.835
It still checks the name and the dates.

59
00:03:27.867 --> 00:03:30.070
Now back to the café question.

60
00:03:30.070 --> 00:03:33.962
Your browser shows a padlock on paypa1-login.example.

61
00:03:33.962 --> 00:03:36.018
Look at that name carefully.

62
00:03:36.018 --> 00:03:37.193
Pause and guess.

63
00:03:37.193 --> 00:03:39.249
What does the padlock prove?

64
00:03:41.433 --> 00:03:46.081
It proves the traffic is sealed, so nobody on the way can read or change it.

65
00:03:46.081 --> 00:03:48.956
And it proves the other end controls that name.

66
00:03:49.167 --> 00:03:49.881
That's all.

67
00:03:49.881 --> 00:03:56.242
Let's Encrypt, a free CA, gives a certificate to anyone who controls a domain, including this one.

68
00:03:56.242 --> 00:03:59.747
The padlock says nothing about whether they're honest.

69
00:03:59.967 --> 00:04:04.517
So the padlock is a very good envelope, and a terrible character reference.

70
00:04:05.333 --> 00:04:06.459
Two more limits.

71
00:04:06.459 --> 00:04:08.428
TLS doesn't hide everything.

72
00:04:08.428 --> 00:04:20.457
Everyone on the path still sees the server's IP address, and usually the host name too, because the browser names the site in a field called SNI, before encryption starts.

73
00:04:20.667 --> 00:04:24.595
And certificates expire, on purpose, sooner each year.

74
00:04:24.595 --> 00:04:29.614
Let's Encrypt certificates last 90 days today, and 45 days from 2028.

75
00:04:29.614 --> 00:04:35.215
Nobody should renew those by hand, so renewal must be automated, and watched.

76
00:04:36.033 --> 00:04:37.707
So, the moral of the story.

77
00:04:37.707 --> 00:04:42.047
TLS seals the envelope on the way, and checks who holds the other end.

78
00:04:42.047 --> 00:04:46.635
Whether the people behind that name deserve your money is still your call.

79
00:04:47.467 --> 00:04:48.555
A quick check.

80
00:04:48.555 --> 00:04:52.755
You pay on tickets.example, over HTTPS, from the café.

81
00:04:52.755 --> 00:04:56.254
What can someone on the café Wi-Fi still see?

82
00:04:56.254 --> 00:04:57.499
Pause and guess.

83
00:04:59.667 --> 00:05:05.113
The site's IP address, and usually its name, because SNI travels before the seal.

84
00:05:05.113 --> 00:05:07.803
But not your password, and not the page.

85
00:05:07.803 --> 00:05:09.820
Those are inside the envelope.
